Ordered roughly by what blocks what. This is the working list.  

---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
1. HOTEND PT1000 REPLACEMENT  [blocks all hotend work]  
---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
The fitted PT1000 has FAILED. Replacement on order, not yet arrived.  
On fitting:  
 - Rebond the sensor into the block. Rutland Furnace Cement or Omega OB-200.  
 - Extend the cable as needed to reach T16/T17 on the HIGH block (TEMP0).  
 - Then run hotend PID autotune — the current M307 model is a placeholder.  
Nothing hotend-side can be commissioned until this is done.  

---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
2. PI F1 FUSE / DISPLAY SHORT  [blocks repowering the Pi + display]  
---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
Root cause CONFIRMED: pogo pins on the back of the Waveshare display short to  
chassis metal when the display is pressed or touched. This blew F1 (3.15A 5V  
MDR, which covers Pi 4, Duet logic and the DSI display) — twice.  

Sequence before repowering, in order:  
 a. Confirm the short is actually cleared (meter across the rail).  
 b. Inspect the pogo pins for damage — they may have taken current.  
 c. Replace F1 (3.15A 5V MDR). Spare needed, see PROCUREMENT.  
 d. Insulate the contact area BEFORE power. Either Kapton tape over the pin  
    area, or a printed standoff/spacer holding the display face clear of the  
    frame with real margin. Prefer the standoff — tape is a wear item here.  

Note while working on the display: this unit's 15-pin FFC cables have contacts  
on the SAME face at both ends, NOT the mirrored/"opposite direction" type that  
generic Pi and Waveshare guides assume. Orientation is consistent at both  
connectors. Confirmed by physical inspection.  

---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
3. CPAP -> PUMP TACH REASSIGNMENT  [verification, quick]  
---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
The plan was: pump tach -> OUT6.tach, CPAP tach dropped (pump flow confirmation  
is more safety-relevant than part-cooling fan RPM). This was deferred to  
execute at pump-wiring time. The pump has since been wired and commissioned,  
but whether the tach swap was actually carried out at the same time is NOT  
confirmed.  

ACTION: check the physical wiring at the board. config.g currently says  
out5.tach for the CPAP. If the swap was done, config.g needs updating to match;  
if it wasn't, decide whether to do it now or leave it.  

