7. GRANITE SLAB  [blocks a large downstream set]  
---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
ORDERED AND PAID (£180, Just Granite Ltd, 01785 711096). Awaiting collection.  
Chase if not heard within a reasonable window.  

Blocks: bed frame geometry lock, M671 coordinates, FR4 hole positions, Z  
endstop configuration, and ANY bed heating.  

BED HEATER WARNING: the active M307 PID model is the calculated one for a 20mm  
granite slab. The bare-mat model has been removed. Running the bed on the bare  
mat with this model will overshoot badly — it expects ~10kg of thermal mass  
that is not physically present. DO NOT RUN THE BED HEATER UNTIL THE SLAB IS  
FITTED.  

ON ARRIVAL, in order:  
 a. Fill the 8x 5mm waterjet through-holes with JB Weld High Heat 37901, then  
    bond the M3 Flow-Sert inserts (Farnell 2474931).  
 b. Bond kinematic brackets to the underside with Dowsil 736 RTV.  
 c. Bond the heater mat full-face with Dowsil 736 — bead/dot grid, NOT a full  
    skim (trapped air voids become hot spots). NOT mechanically retained.  
 d. Drill the FR4 FROM THE GRANITE PATTERN DIRECTLY, do not mark out  
    independently. 2.5mm pilot then open to 4.5mm. Sacrificial ply backing.  
 e. Lock bed frame geometry, set M671 (Z0 front-left, Z1 rear, Z2 front-right  
    — the M671 triples must be in that order).  
 f. Reduce Z max in config.g by 1mm for the FR4.  
 g. Only then run the bed heater.  

---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
8. BED MOUNTING METHOD — UNDECIDED  
---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
Two candidates:  
 a. RTV-bonded kinematic brackets.  
 b. Three silicone pads (vertical support and compliance) plus a rear  
    locating pin.  

Open problem with (b): friction alone cannot both resist twist and permit  
thermal creep at the same pad. Those are contradictory requirements.  

Last idea on the table: keep the silicone pads for vertical support and  
compliance, and add a printed GEOMETRIC constraint — a shallow groove or tabs  
— at the front two pads to fix rotation, while leaving the radial direction  
free for frame expansion. Geometric, not frictional.  

NOT DECIDED. Settle this before the slab arrives if possible, since (a) and  
(b) imply different bonding steps at fit-up.  

