---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
11. UMBILICAL CABLE SUPPORT  [waiting on parts]  
---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
SETTLED APPROACH: fixed anchor point through a hole in the top cover, centred  
over the bed travel envelope. Worst-case diagonal reach sets the required  
slack / cord length.  

Boom design (fixed pivot arm off cross-drilled 2020 + bearings) REJECTED — no  
travel clearance for a swept arm given toolhead reach into the corners.  

Penetration: cut oversize through the aluminium+PIR stack, foil-tape at the  
PIR edge, printed grommet/bushing to prevent chafing on the cut aluminium.  
Same treatment as other panel penetrations — NOT a sealed gland.  

Cord: Key-Bak retractable reel, clipped or zip-tied to the umbilical bundle  
sleeve every 50-75mm so the bundle hangs in a shallow curve rather than  
loading at a single point.  

PART CHANGE: the 13oz reel already in hand is TOO STRONG for this load.  
Replaced by the Key-Bak Sidekick — 4oz retraction force, Kevlar cord  
(heat-tolerant for the chamber-side segment). ORDERED, awaiting arrival.  

Bundle carries: CPAP cable, water lines (in + out), PTFE tube. Water lines  
confirmed NOT heat-sensitive at this duty (closed loop, radiator-cooled), so  
there is no PU tube temperature-rating concern.  

---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
12. WATER LOOP PLUMBING AND PRESSURE TEST  [independent of granite]  
---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
Pump, reservoir and radiator are mounted; the loop is not yet plumbed.  

Loop order: pump outlet -> Goliath block -> NEMA14 motor plate -> rad top in  
-> rad bottom out -> reservoir -> pump.  

Do this BEFORE the machine is fully buttoned up. A leak found on the bench is  
much better than one found inside a finished chamber. Estimated fill ~0.9-1L,  
Mayhems X1 Clear Premix (contains its own biocide — do not add more).  

Set the pump dial to the desired flow rate and leave it; there is no PWM  
speed control.  

---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
13. DOOR SEAL COMPARISON  [bench test, parts in hand]  
---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
Frame is built and glazed, so the comparison can now be run. Two candidates:  
 a. 10x3mm silicone foam tape on the frame face.  
 b. Silicone tube cord in the T-slot. Received in two bores, OD10xID6 and  
    OD10xID8, 3m each, to test compression fit.  
Not yet run.  

---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
14. FILAMENT HANDLING — UNDECIDED  
---------------------------------------------------------------------------  
Three options, no decision. IO cost is the deciding constraint: only IO0 and  
IO4 are free, with IO3 reserved for a runout sensor.  

 a. Mellow filament buffer board. Dedicated onboard MCU with its own  
    closed-loop buffer control. IO cost 1 (runout on IO3), rising to 3 if  
    FEED/RETRACT button-to-host signalling is also wired — which would consume  
    every remaining free IO.  

 b. Duet rotating-magnet filament monitor. Jam and under-extrusion detection  
    only; does NOT address feed-path drag, which is the actual problem.  
    IO cost 1 (fits IO4).  

 c. DIY buffer. Mobius 4 (Voron) frame-mounted geared extruder as the buffer  
    feed motor, driven via RRF's M567 tool mixing-ratio — NOT M584  
    axis-bonding. Mixing ratios apply per extruder drive independently, each  
    keeping its own M92, so there is no forced-identical-drivetrain  
    constraint. Concept ported from Klipper/Box Turtle AFC's bang-bang  
    gear-ratio correction: a baseline mix ratio absorbs any gross drivetrain  
    mismatch, and two limit switches on the buffer mechanism trigger a small  
    swing above and below baseline (e.g. 0.95/1.05, or an offset from any  
    baseline such as 1.25/1.35) to correct drift.  
    IO cost 3 (2x buffer limit switches + 1x runout) — fits exactly in  
    IO0/IO3/IO4 with nothing spare. Conceptual only; mechanism not sized or  
    built.  

